nd into the  20s and the 30s and and creating  different kinds of settings that were  more and more naturalistic as the  equipment got a little bit more flexible  and as the artistry got more  as the artistry got more artistic some  of the techniques that were evolved in  the 30s for instance and the 20s are  basically still the same techniques that  we have now that we use now only we use  computers instead of little miniatures  or glass shots or different things but  ultimately the thing that hasn't changed  is that it's an image image driven art  it the each image on the screen forwards  the story and pulls the pose the  listener pulls the audience in to the  world of the story and and that hasn't  changed whereas in the in the 30s you  would have a foreground constructed  space and perhaps miniatures or some  kind of ambien middle ground and then  you would have a flat painted backdrop  in the background now we use computer  technology to create either computer  match shots or three-dimensional match  shots we make it seem more real but it's  the same technique and and really what  has had to evolve for art directors and  for production designers is that we've  had to know what the not only what the  uses of these new techniques are and  what their limitations are but we also  have to know how to blend them and and  use a live action when necessary in  certain limited ways so that it will  seamlessly blend into whatever technique  it is we're using to extend or create  that world  you
 

